{
  "email": "tom.becker@brightline.example",
  "reason": "Find the quarantined scan email with zip attachment"
}

{
  "user_id": 4,
  "reason": "Account is locked with 5 failed sign-ins and user received a malware-flagged email; check for suspicious activity"
}

{
  "summary": "Reported: Tom Becker (Finance, user_id 4) asked us to release a quarantined email with a scanned-document zip. He thinks it came from the office copier. Found: the message is Q-1005, sender scanner@docs-share.example, subject \"Scanned document 0931.zip\", received 2026-10-08 09:08 UTC, quarantine reason MALWARE, releasable=false. The sender is an external domain, not an internal copier, which fits a typical fake scan-to-email lure. I did NOT release it (KB-007/KB-008). The message never reached his inbox, so there is no sign he opened it. Also found: his account is currently LOCKED after 5 failed password attempts on 2026-10-09 between 14:28 and 14:43 UTC. All attempts came from the corporate IP 203.0.113.10 (New York, his usual network). There are no hosting-network or foreign sign-ins, no MFA denials, and his recent successful sign-ins look normal with Duo. This looks like the user mistyping his password, not an attack, but please keep it in mind. He did not ask for an unlock, so none is queued. Still needed: security review of sender docs-share.example and a block for all users if appropriate, a check of whether other users got the same lure, and confirmation of where the copier really sends scans. Separately: Q-1004 (Harborview Supplies invoice, spam, releasable) is also in his quarantine. I did not release it because he hasn't confirmed he expects it.",
  "reason": "User wants a malware-flagged message released; KB-007/KB-008 say never release and escalate for security review of the sender"
}

{
  "outcome": "escalated",
  "reply_to_user": "Hi Tom, I'm sorry, but I can't release that email. Our filters flagged the \"Scanned document 0931.zip\" message as malware. It also came from an outside address (scanner@docs-share.example), not from our office copier. Fake \"scanned document\" emails are a common way scammers spread malware. It hasn't reached your inbox, so you're safe. I've passed it to our security team to check the sender and block it if needed. If you're waiting on real scans, please try scanning again at the copier, and let us know if they still don't show up so we can check the copier's email settings. Two other things: 1) Your account is currently locked after several wrong password attempts this morning. Reply if you need it unlocked. 2) There's also an invoice from Harborview Supplies (#4471) in quarantine. If you're expecting it, tell me and I can ask a technician to release it. Thanks!",
  "internal_note": "Quarantine check: Q-1005 from scanner@docs-share.example, subject \"Scanned document 0931.zip\", reason MALWARE, not releasable. Not released (KB-007/KB-008). The sender is an external domain, not an internal copier, which fits a fake scan lure. The message was quarantined, so the user never received it and there's no sign it was opened. Escalated to Tier 2/security (escalation 1) to review and block the sender. Account: LOCKED after 5 failed passwords on 10/09 between 14:28 and 14:43 UTC, all from the corporate IP 203.0.113.10 in New York. No hosting-network or foreign sign-ins, no MFA denials; probably user error. No unlock queued because the user didn't ask; I offered one in the reply. Q-1004 (Harborview invoice, spam, releasable) not released pending user confirmation.",
  "kb_articles": [
    "KB-007",
    "KB-008"
  ],
  "confidence": "high"
}
